rdacDLCdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  ae-sz---0http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/geographicAreas/e-sz  ae-gx---0http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/geographicAreas/e-gx  aXZL40 aD 110.18:L 821 aGordon, Alex Thomas,eauthor.4http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/relators/aut0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no202504159814aThe Alpine arsenal :bSwiss logistics and preparation for war from 1933 to 1941 /cAlex Thomas Gordon.30aSwiss logistics and preparation for war from 1933 to 1941 1aFort Leavenworth, KS :bArmy University Press, US Army Combined Arms Center,c2025.  a1 online resource (vii, 114 pages).  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aArt of war papers  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I); Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP) distribution status to be determined upon publication.  aIncludes bibliographical references.  a"Swiss World War II military history has been neglected in English-language historiography because Switzerland successfully remained neutral and other controversial topics regarding the Swiss experience during the war have dominated the historical conversation. After the destabilizing rise of Nazism in Germany in 1933, Switzerland's survival was seriously threatened by the events unfolding in Europe and required significant military preparation. This manuscript fills that historiographic gap with discussion on Swiss military logistics preparation from 1933 to 1941. Due to Swiss preparation in the form of mobilization, rearmament, and logistics force design Switzerland's logistical preparation was complete by 1941 which contributed to its ability to remain neutral during World War II. This success was due to the close alignment of Swiss preparation with their various military strategies, operational environment, and unique military traditions. Small nations that are near larger and increasingly aggressive neighbors during today's era of great power competition should study the Swiss experience during World War II to inform their own security preparation"-- Provided by publisher.  aDescription based on print version record and CIP data provided by publisher; resource not viewed.10aSwitzerland.bArméexLogisticsxHistory.10aSwitzerland.bArméexMobilizationxHistory.10aSwitzerland.bArméexTransportationxHistory. 0aLogisticsxHistory. 0aWorld War, 1939-1945xLogisticszSwitzerland. 0aSwitzerlandxDefensesxHistory. 0aSwitzerlandxMilitary policyy20th century. 0aGermanyxMilitary policyy20th century.2 aU.S. Army Combined Arms Center,eissuing body.1http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no20040936704http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/relators/isb0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200409367008iPrint version:aGordon, Alex Thomas.tAlpine arsenaldFort Leavenworth, KS : Army University Press, US Army Combined Arms Center, 2025z9781737204657w(DLC) 2025001787 0aArt of war papers.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20130830581http://id.loc.gov/resources/hubs/5c539c92-d658-150d-10d0-38f165e47c6640uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo258809704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/Research%20and%20Books/AoW/The-Alpine-Arsenal/AOW%20Swiss%20Logistics%20Gordon.pdf  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806  aBIBCONEWb20260806  aVEND01b2026082602050cam a2200421 i 00147754720260806141820.0m     o  d f      241015s2024    vauab   ob   f000 0 eng d  a(OCoLC)1460995492  aXRVbengerdaepncXRVdOCLCOdOCLCLdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdGPO  aXZL4  a0624-A (online)0 aI 19.15/5:16-B 21 aScully, Rebecca A.,eauthor.12aA data exchange standard for wadeable stream habitat monitoring data /cBy Rebecca A. Scully [and 10 others]. 1aReston, Virginia :bU.S. Department of the Interior, U.S. Geological Survey,c2024.  a1 online resource (vii, 28 pages) :billustrations, maps.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamed