pe of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 apt. 1 The winged bean, Psophocarpus tetragonolobus--pt. 2. Okra, Abelmoschus esculentus--pt. 3. Chaya, Cnidoscolus chayamansa--pt. 4. Sponge and bottle gourds, Luffa and Lagenaria--pt. 5. Eggplant, Solanum melongena--pt. 6. Amaranth and celosia, Amaranthus and Celosia--pt. 7. The peppers, Capsicum species--pt. 8. Vegetable corns, Zea mays.  aMicrofiche.bWashington, D.C.,cU.S. G.P.O.,d1981.e5 microfiches : negative ; 11 x 15 cm.0 aPt. 1, 3-6. 0aTropical crops.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85138053 0aVegetableszTropics. 7aTropical crops.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01157417 7aVegetables.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01164784 7aTropics.2fast0(OCoLC)fst012406741 aUnited States.bAgricultural Research Service.bSouthern Region.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n780372731 aUnited States.bScience and Education Administration.bOffice of the Regional Administrator for Federal Research (Southern Region)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n791288991 aUnited States.bScience and Education Administration.bAgricultural Research.bSouthern Region.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n791420792 aMayagüez Institute of Tropical Agriculture.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n896427180 a.Winged bean, Psophocarpus tetragonolobus.0 aPeppers, Capsicum species.0 aVegetable corns, Zea mays.0 aAmaranth and celosia, Amaranthus and Celosia.0 aEggplant, Solanum melongena.0 aSponge and bottle gourds, Luffa and Lagenaria.0 aChaya Cnidoscolus chayamansa.0 aOkra, Abelmoschus esculentus.08iPrint version:w(OCoLC)4409505  aC0bGPO  aHistoric Shelflist Drawer 68; HSL002;c20260817  aVEND01b2026082603708cam a2200553 i 00147801820260817124257.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||150710e198602  vau     ot   f000 0 eng d  a(OCoLC)831847065  aDTICEbengerdaepncDTICEdOCLCQdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aXZL4  a0330-E-4 (online)0 aD 101.60/9:86-171 aShake, Leasa G.,eauthor.10aTen click rule :ba test of the effectiveness of a new rule for simplifying battle field sight adjustment /cLeasa G. Shake and James E. Schroeder. 1aAlexandria, Virginia :bU.S. Army Research Institute for the Behavioral and Social Sciences,cFebruary 1986.  a1 online resource (ii, 12 pages).  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aResearch Note ;v86-10  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  a"January 1986."  a"ARI Field Unit at Fort Benning, Georgia."  a"Training Research Laboratory, Seward Smith, Acting Director."  aIncludes bibliographical references (page 10).  d2Q263743A794  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover page (DTIC, viewed August 17, 2026).8 aSoldiers must be able to adjust the sights of their weapon in the field without the use of the zero target. The 10-click rule is only one possible alternate method to the range-computation method that could accomplish this. Others could include more extensive training using the present method, more actual live-fire practicing adjusting sights, or more field sight adjustment exercises; however, many of these may not be very cost effective. If a training rule is to be developed, there are certain needs that must be met in order to have an effective rule. This rule must be comprehendable, easy to explain, concise, quickly executable, and most important, it must be accurate. The 10- click rule has the potential to satisfy all these needs. Although this research was conducted with a small number of subjects, it does reflect support for further investigation into using the 10-click rule as a replacement to the presently used range-computation formula. Keywords: Markmanship; M16A1 rifle.  aApproved for public release; distribution is unlimited.  aEnglish10aUnited States.bArmyxPersonnel management.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85140687 0aShootingzUnited States. 0aRiflesxSightszUnited States.1 aSchroeder, James E.,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n881270602 aU.S. Army Research Institute for the Behavioral and Social Sciences.bARI Field Unit at Fort Benning,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8203570508iPrint version:tTen Click Rule: A Test of the Effectiveness of a New Rule for Simplifying Battle Field Sight Adjustmentw(OCoLC)227679115 0aARI research note ;v86-17.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no0007884740uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo79747704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttp://www.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a168797.pdf  aC0bGPO  aBIBCONEWb20260817  abc76 20260817b20260817  aVEND01b2026082602325cam a2200457 a 00147802320260817141815.0m     o  d        cr bn||||||abpcr bn||||||ada120702s1977    dcua    o    f000 0 eng d  a799686633a960103876a1128131362  a(OCoLC)798012693z(OCoLC)799686633z(OCoLC)960103876z(OCoLC)1128131362  aOCLCEbengepncOCLCEdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCAdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLdOCLCQdOCLCAdOCLCQdNJRdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCLdOCLCOdOCLCQdGPO  adlr  ae-it---an-us---  aXZL4  a1039-C (online)0 aY 4.F 76/2:IT 1/31 aPell, Claiborne,d1918-2009.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911295510aItaly at a critical crossroads in her history :ba report /cby Senator Claiborne Pell to the Committee on Foreign Relations, United States Senate, on his trip to Italy, November 1976. 1aWashington :bU.S. Govt. Print. Off.,c1977.  a1 online resource (vii, 20 pages) :billustrations  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aAt head of title: 95th Congress, 1st session. Committee print.0 aPrint version record. 0aItalyxPolitics and governmenty1945-1976.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8506901520aPartito comunista italiano.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7900646520aPartito della democrazia cristiana.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79006461 0aItalyxForeign relationszUnited States. 0aUnited StatesxForeign relationszItaly. 0aEarthquakeszItalyzUdine (Province)1 aUnited States.bCongress.bSenate.bCommittee on Foreign Relations.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7800823008iPrint version:aPell, Claiborne, 1918-2009.tItaly at a critical crossroads in her historyw(DLC) 77601297w(OCoLC)298300040uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo259151704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CPRT-95SPRT82130O/pdf/CPRT-95SPRT82130O.pdf  aC0bGPO  abca68 20260817b20260817  aVEND01b2026082604280cam a2200613 i 00147803320260817152120.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||150710e198604  vaua    obt  f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)834227529  aDTICEbengerdaepncDTICEdOCLCQdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aXZL4  a0330-E-4 (online)0 aD 101.60/9:86-33  aAD-A167 3971 aRouse, William B.,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8104582410aHuman problem solving in fault diagnosis tasks /cWilliam B. Rouse and Ruston M. Hunt. 1aAlexandria, Virginia :bU.S. Army Research Institute for the Behavioral and Social Sciences,cApril 1986.  a1 online resource (iii, 50 pages) :billustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aResearch Note ;v86-33  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  a"April 1986."  a"Center for Man-Machine Systems Research, Georgia Institute of Technology."  a"Contracting Officer's Representative, Michael Drillings; Basic Research, Milton S. Katz, Director".  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 47-50).  aThis report evaluates the nature of human problem solving abilities specifically related to fault diagnosis situations. Three types of fault diagnosis were analysed involving diagnosis utilizing both real equipment and computer simulated equipment failures. In addition, the investigators experimented with computer generated problem solving aids to supplement human decision making capacities in diagnostic tasks. Results of the initial investigation indicate that human problem solving tends to be highly context- specific but that pattern recognition capabilities are exceptional allowing for a high degree of accuracy in ambiguous problem solving situations. Both structured-oriented and strategy-oriented problem solving aids were analysed. Structured-oriented bookkeeping aids clearly improved performance, while strategy-oriented aids actually had a negative effect on transfer of training. This research effort is clearly relevant to military interests in effective training of optimal decision making in sub-optimal conditions. The results strongly support further research into the nature and training of effective solving. Keywords: Troubleshooting; Fuzzy set model; Complexity model; Rule- based model; Training.  cMDA 903-79-C-0421d2Q161102B74F  aApproved for public release; distribution is unlimited.  aEnglish  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover page (DTIC, viewed August 17, 2026).10aUnited States.bArmyxPersonnel management.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85140687 0aSleep deprivation.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85123403 0aTrainingzUnited States. 0aProblem solvingzUnited States. 0aFuzzy sets.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85052627 0aRule-based programming.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh88003529  aPE61102A1 aHunt, Ruston McClellan,d1955-eauthor.0http